{
  "narrative_body": "Evika Siliņa, the prime minister, told her coalition partners this week that she was ready to let the government fall rather than yield over a €30 million state loan to airBaltic — and came close to doing exactly that.\n\nThe Union of Greens and Farmers (ZZS), one of New Unity's coalition partners, issued an ultimatum: remove the transport minister or lose ZZS support for the loan. Ms Siliņa refused. After crisis talks on April 16, the partners stepped back. The Saeima approved the loan; Transport Minister Kaspars Švinka kept his post on condition he deliver a path to profitability; Ms Siliņa ordered a reorganization of his ministry. An Economics Ministry assessment showed why the government believed it could not let airBaltic fail: the airline accounts for 57% of Riga airport traffic and generated €779 million in revenue in 2025, despite a €44 million net loss. Edgars Rinkēvičs, the president, called for serious restructuring and declined to comment on the loan.\n\nThe airBaltic row might have passed as a single bad week. What prevented that was the simultaneous accumulation of two other accountability actions — each individually manageable, together amounting to something harder to shake off. Latvia's anti-corruption bureau, KNAB, announced it would assess allegations that Ms Siliņa spent €4,184 of state money on VIP lounge use at Amsterdam Schiphol during a 2024 trip to New York — allegations made by Jānis Citskovskis, the former head of the State Chancellery and himself facing a criminal case, whom Ms Siliņa promptly threatened to sue for defamation. Mr Rinkēvičs defended VIP lounge use by senior officials on security grounds but said Ms Siliņa should explain her own case. KNAB is also examining whether a three-day seminar that Ms Siliņa and other New Unity ministers attended at a luxury villa on Lake Como — taken as personal vacation and hosted by the Konrad Adenauer Foundation — constitutes an impermissible in-kind gift to a political party. Political analyst Iveta Kažoka, writing for LSM, found that a governing style now \"indelibly associated with privileges\" was eroding support for both New Unity and ZZS, with more than a quarter of voters still undecided and newer parties drawing in the disillusioned. ZZS, she found, is in the worse position — at risk of losing its core vote.\n\nNone of this amounts to institutional failure. KNAB is investigating. The president is pressing. The Saeima passed new anti-SLAPP legislation this week, implementing an EU directive to protect journalists, civic activists, and researchers from abusive litigation — a law whose protections for public-interest speech arrive with particular irony given Ms Siliņa's threat to sue her accuser. The institutions are working. But the governing coalition's political capital is draining, six months before Latvia votes on October 3.\n\nOne dimension of that pressure now touches election infrastructure directly. A corruption case investigated by the European Public Prosecutor's Office — which led to the arrest of 21 people, including the former director of the State Information Network Agency, on IT procurement fraud charges — has raised doubts about the integrity of Latvia's online voter register. Mr Rinkēvičs set a deadline of early May for authorities to confirm the system works or produce a clear backup plan. The Saeima has already adopted manual ballot counting for October. The State Chancellery submitted a classified report to Cabinet on the inter-institutional group coordinating election security. The seriousness of the threat is being taken seriously.\n\nA stranger security-governance episode also surfaced this week. Aleksejs Rosļikovs, a Riga city councilman who fled to Belarus after being charged with inciting national hatred, remotely attended a city council committee meeting from Minsk, casting abstention votes on every item. The city's mayor moved immediately to block remote participation from non-EU and non-NATO states. An international arrest warrant is in force.\n\nLatvia looked outward too, and further than usual. After a Baltic prime ministers' summit in Tallinn — where Ms Siliņa reaffirmed the 2030 Rail Baltica completion target while acknowledging Latvia's funding shortfalls and admitting construction would span several governments — she and Estonian Prime Minister Kristen Michal joined a video call hosted by Emmanuel Macron and Keir Starmer on freedom of navigation through the Strait of Hormuz. Latvia has no navy for such a mission, but Ms Siliņa expressed support for an international coalition and stressed the need to involve the United States. It is the clearest signal yet of Latvia's willingness to engage security questions beyond its immediate neighborhood. Economics Minister Viktors Valainis separately led a business delegation to Warsaw and Kyiv for defence-technology and energy cooperation; Poland is Latvia's fourth-largest trade partner, with €3.94 billion in combined trade in 2024, while Ukraine-Latvia trade totalled €724 million.\n\nBeneath the political turbulence, the fiscal picture is quietly darkening. Latvia's Fiscal Discipline Council warned this week that without new revenue measures or spending cuts, fiscal space could turn negative by 2028. Debt servicing is projected to rise from 1.3% of GDP now to 1.6% by 2029-2030, reaching €901 million a year. The main driver is defence: Latvia plans to spend above 5% of GDP from 2027, generating deficits of 3-5% of GDP, while pension obligations, Rail Baltica, airBaltic, and health and education commitments are not fully captured in current forecasts. The Iran conflict adds energy price risk on top. The Cabinet approved one limited response this week — a law imposing a 100% levy on fuel retail margins more than 3% above a weekly government reference price, a tool that can be switched on by Cabinet order and expires at the end of 2026.",
  "other_stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Siliņa's Amsterdam airport VIP lounge use sparks political scandal; KNAB opens review",
      "summary": "Former State Chancellery Director Jānis Citskovskis publicly accused PM Siliņa of unjustified use of the VIP lounge at Amsterdam Schiphol airport during a 2024 US visit, at a cost of roughly €4,184. Siliņa accused Citskovskis of defamation and threatened legal action, calling his statements a criminal defence tactic (Citskovskis himself faces a criminal case). KNAB announced it would review the allegations against both Siliņa and former PM Kariņš (whose daughter also used a VIP lounge at state expense, though costs were later deducted from his salary). President Rinkēvičs defended VIP lounge use by top officials on security grounds but declined to comment on Siliņa's specific case directly."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Party ratings fall, public trust in coalition erodes ahead of October elections",
      "summary": "Analysis of the latest Latvian party polling shows deepening public dissatisfaction, a large pool of undecided voters, and a broad sense that recent coalition scandals — Italy seminar, airBaltic, VIP lounge — have accumulated into a 'privilege aura' damaging New Unity. President Rinkēvičs separately observed that an unusually sharp pre-election battle for voters was already underway, with many voters disillusioned with their former choices."
    },
    {
      "headline": "President Rinkēvičs urges faster access to oncology and diabetes medicines",
      "summary": "President Rinkēvičs met with patient activists and publicly called on the Health Ministry, government and Saeima to step-by-step address the availability of innovative medicines for cancer and diabetes patients. PM Siliņa told him that work was underway; Rinkēvičs said additional budget of around €150 million was being sought for 80 drugs."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sweden warns NATO that Russia may attempt to seize a Baltic Sea island as a provocation",
      "summary": "Sweden's armed forces commander Mikael Klaesson warned in an interview with The Times that Russia could attempt to seize a Baltic Sea island in the near term to test NATO's reaction, and that a war conclusion in Ukraine would not mean peace in Europe — instead Russia would regroup and redirect resources westward. Latvian outlets picked up the warning."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Police plan warning letters to 31 Latvians for using harmful online platform",
      "summary": "Latvia's State Police announced plans to send preventive warning letters to 31 residents for using a platform designated as harmful on the internet. No further details about the platform were provided in available snippets."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rinkēvičs demands two-week deadline to resolve electronic voter register questions ahead of October elections",
      "summary": "President Rinkēvičs set a deadline of early May for authorities to clarify the functioning of Latvia's online voter registration system and to have a clear alternative plan ready if concerns arise, ahead of the October 3 Saeima elections. He stressed that election legitimacy is the main priority."
    }
  ]
}

